          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+derived+functor"} right derived functor
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            is called the
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ext
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext
              \-functor
            \. It derives its name from the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom-functor"} derived hom-functor
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            classifies abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extensions"} group extensions
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+extensions"} ∞-group extensions
            by general
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
          %p
            Together with the
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tor"} Tor
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            we may consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{\mathcal{A}} : \mathcal{A}^{op}\times \mathcal{A}\to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            either as a functor in first or in second argument, and compute the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+derived+functors"} right derived functors
          %p
            If they exist, the classical right derived functors of either functor agree and also agree with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            of the mixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
            obtained by taking simultaneously a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolution"} projective resolution
            of the first contravariant argument and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
            of the second covariant argument. The last construction is called the
            %em
              balanced
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext
          %p
            Alternatively, one can consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(\mathcal{A})
            and define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ext
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ext^p(X,A) \coloneqq Hom_{D(A)}(X,A[p])
          %p
            or define
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
